Date: Fri, 8 Oct 93 0:47:18 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Zen of Perry

Four koans from the Zen mind of Perry:

> Violence accomplishes little or nothing. If a revolution comes, it
> will be largely in people's heads. 


> The most effective strategy to end the tyranny of the state is the zen
> strategy. Ask yourself the next time you are mad about what the
> government is doing to you, "Who is the Buddha that makes the grass
> green?" To be less cryptic, "who is the guy cooperating with them when
> he could be ignoring them. Not fighting -- ignoring."


> Overseas cryptobanks are an example of methodologies for ignoring the
> state.


> When you fight something, you aren't free of it. Since the state is a
> consentual hallucination, cease to consent, and you will be free.


If this is the new Perry, accompanied by Tibetan singing bowls, I
really like it! Maybe Perry's vegetarian diet has induced him to
embrace a kind of crypto-Gandhiism, nonviolence with a beatifc smile.
Om's Law, as it were.

By Tribble's Law, I must indicate seriousness or satire. I opt for
seriousness. 

Perry's advice is sound. I was of course not advocating violence, just
laying out my "trans-moralist" philosophy, in which human life is not
"sacred," just another factor. After all, if a million Bengalis die
when the waters inevitably rise, do we really claim an incalcuable
disaster has occurred?

And I'm a fan of Harry Browne's "How I Found Freedom in an Unfree
World," one of the sacred texts of the zenarcho-capitalists.

-Crypto-zenarcho-capitalist Initiate Tim

P.S. Weren't the Zen "koans" really the source of the Cohens, one of
the main tribes of Israel? Zen Master Perry has inside knowledge,
perhaps?




