Date: Fri, 8 Oct 93 00:48:44 -0700
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Inconceivable!

(That subject line is for those of you who've seen "The Princess
Bride" -- you'll see why in a minute).

Background first:

A couple of days ago, someone at work sent around a message that had
been forwarded to him by a friend at Sun.  The message was a long one
(260 lines) from a Sun employee in Moscow who was close enough to the
fighting to have to duck flying debris, and who typed in his
up-to-the-minute coverage for the benefit of those of us stateside.

This afternoon, the following message came around from the VP in
charge of our division (names deleted in a possibly vain attempt to
reduce my "exposure"):

----- Begin Included Message -----
>From XXXXX@XXXXXXXX Thu Oct  7 17:41:35 1993
From: XXXXX@XXXXXXXX (XXX XXXXX)
To: XXXXXXXX
Subject: Re: fwd>>Another Day in Moscow
Content-Length: 133
X-Lines: 3

I trust that comsumption of this type of material is not causing too much
of a distraction from the task in hand here at XXXXXXXX !?
XXX

----- End Included Message -----

After gaping at it in disbelief for a few minutes, I mentally composed
and rejected four or so different replies (each one a career-limiting
maneuver), and proceeded to gripe at length in the general direction
of my friends at work (who didn't like it either, but weren't inclined
to say so).

Mind you, we get maybe one message like this every two or three weeks,
and most people work 10-12 hour days.

So why am I posting this here?  Because I'm interested in diverse
input from an Extropian standpoint, not because the topic itself has
anything to do with Extropianism (except insofar as everything does,
but that's another discussion).

Part of me says "Get out while you still can".  Another part says
"Stick it out -- they're paying you good money, and you're learning
new marketable skills -- besides, the job market sucks".  I'm
interested in your insights.  For example, have you had experience
with this sort of brain-damaged attitude?  What's worked (and not
worked) for you?

It may be most appropriate to take further discussion to email.

Still mad, and finding it hard to remain Dynamically Optimistic,
Kennita

