Date: Fri, 8 Oct 1993 9:00:18 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: burglary survival/civil liberties

Tim May writes:

>In my 41 years, no burglaries, except once when my car was broken into
>and my stereo stolen, about 15 years ago. My sister and brother have
>never been burgled, to my knowledge, nor have my parents.

>All totaled, zero home burglaries in perhaps 80 household-years.

This is not so improbable.

The Home Office in the UK quotes an official rate of one burglary
per 20 household-years on _average_. So you're not far out of line
even in the _relatively_ burglary-ridden UK.

>Nor do I read about many home burglaries, certainly nothing
>approaching the incredible frequency Charlies Stross described. It
>makes me think that Britain treats casual burglaries as just
>supplemental income for those on the dole.

Hardly! I'm just an anomaly -- I had the misfortune to be in the top
percentile of burgled households. As I indicated, in the ten years
since moving out of that house, the four households in my immediate
family have been burgled _once_; back down to the national average, or
thereabouts. 

The British government don't take it lightly, either. In fact, I'm
afraid they take crime too seriously -- seeing it as something to be
tackled in a 'war', like the'war on [some] drugs', rather than as a
symptom. As witness the current Conservative party conference, at
which they promised a fairly massive clamp-down on crime. Oh yes:
three fairly frightening tid-bits buried in the get-tough rhetoric have
put the wind up me.

*       Abolition of the right to silence. (This is the right of a
        suspect to refuse to answer police or court questions without
        prejudice.) Yes, they're abolishing it. If you won't incriminate
        yourself you must be guilty. In US political terms this is
        equivalent to abolishing the 5th Amendment, but it's gone down
        with no more than a peep over here.

*       Abolition of the right to a jury trial in all criminal cases.
        Yup, you no longer have the automatic right to a trial by your
        peers in the UK, when this Bill goes through.

and finally, most ominously, a catch-all:

*       To make posession of any article that might be used to further
        a terrorist offense a crime. This could be interpreted as
        'ten years for posession of a copy of pgp.'

This little lot of draconian nonsense is buried in an omnibus bill to
'get tough on crime' -- a standard Conservative bugaboo that comes up
every year -- and I don't think enough people understand what it
means. The UK is relatively unhysterical about drugs; they're illegal
and the police spend a lot of time on them, but the level of ranting
is far lower than in the US. However, the government has staked a lot
on getting everybody chained into their petty-beourgeois vision of a
home-owning democracy, and is becoming increasingly alarmed that its
unpopularity will increase if they can't protect the home-owners
they've created from the depradations of the new underclass. (Which
didn't exist before they came into office -- odd, that!)

The war on burglary and theft occupies the same place in Tory ideology
that the War on Drugs does in US political discourse. And in the same
way, it's used as an excuse for sweeping restrictions on civil
liberties.

Q: would you accept a higher risk of having your house burgled, in
return for guaranteed civil rights and freedoms? I know I would. Being
burgled is unpleasant. Being imprisoned for dissidence is, IMHO,
worse.



-- Charlie

