Date: Fri, 8 Oct 1993 9:26:47 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: GUNS, SOC: Burglary and countermeasures

Peter Cappello writes:
[lost quote!]
>>I think I would much rather have my stereo, computer, etc., rather  
>>than give them up, even at the cost of the burglar's life, ...

>While I enjoyed this post very much, especially the part about  
>stealing part of one's past life, I wonder where the proviso "let the  
>punishment fit the crime" fits into this?  My intuition is that  
>deviating from this wisdom has its own downside.  If my intuition is  
>correct, then the spontaneous order induced (via its incentive  
>structure) by killing VCR thiefs may deviate from the one we might  
>most prefer.

I agree.

I've seen quite a lot of rhetoric advocating shooting people who break
into your home.  I think this is a disproportionate response to an
unarmed intrusion.  "Let the punishment fit the crime" by all means.
If I go home tonight and find some asshole in my flat, then I would
probably try to whack them over the head with the heaviest object that
comes to hand, and maybe bring their ribs into acquaintance with my
boots before calling the police.  I get _really_peeved_ when property
that I worked hard to buy or that holds sentimental value for me is
stolen.  Please don't get the idea from my earlier posting that I'm in
favour of letting burglars get away with whatever they want ...

Still, killing out-of-hand for a crime against property someone who
hasn't threatened _me_ with violence or death strikes me as being a
wee bit out of proportion.  Indeed, it's on the same moral level as
the Bloody Code, the law of England in the 18th century, that
stipulated death by hanging for the theft of a loaf of bread -- even
if the thief was demonstrably at the point of starvation.

Let's keep a sense of proportion.  Getting violent in self-defense is
one thing.  Getting violent in defense of a life's work is another.
But getting violent in defense of, say, a five-year-old VCR is _maybe_
not quite so easily justified.



-- Charlie
